00:16:24,581 --> 00:16:28,380
It simply was an area that livestock grazed and that

182
00:16:28,381 --> 00:16:36,280
That grazing kept the grass short, and that short crop grass is really the genesis of lawns.

183
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:40,780
Prior to the 16th century, there were a few lawns in Europe.

184
00:16:43,180 --> 00:16:46,940
Pastures and public green spaces contained a smorgasbord of grass varieties.

185
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:50,960
The grass types were quite different than what we see here.

186
00:16:50,961 --> 00:16:58,400
In fact, some of the grasses, or early lawns rather, were actually things like chamomile or clover and meadow grasses.

187
00:16:59,060 --> 00:17:02,380
And these were things that were native to northern Europe at the time.

188
00:17:03,720 --> 00:17:12,620
French landscape architect André Lenotre designed one of the first ornamental lawns in 1661 at the Gardens of Versailles near Paris.

189
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:20,340
Soon after, British noblemen began planting formal English gardens.

190
00:17:20,960 --> 00:17:27,360
But for the English peasant class, the lawn was still an impractical dream.

191
00:17:28,740 --> 00:17:32,000
The grass wasn't necessarily greener in the American colonies.

192
00:17:33,280 --> 00:17:40,020
Native grasses like broom straw and wild rye didn't have the nutrients to sustain the grazing animals brought from Europe.

193
00:17:40,760 --> 00:17:50,660
